{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json; rg -n 'json.pointer|json_pointer|LOCATOR|#/' scientific-evidence/spec.md | head -20; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json'))\nfor n in ['RichFigureManifestFigure','RichFigurePanel']:\n d=s['\"'$defs'\"'][n]; print(n,'required',d.get('required'));print('fields',list(d['properties']))\nPY\nsed -n '1,85p' paper_strategy.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"$id\": \"https://schemas.agentic-scientist.local/scientific-evidence/evidence-reference.schema.json\",\n  \"$schema\": \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"locator\": {\n      \"description\": \"Opaque locator fragment after #. The base verifier does not need universal parsing.\",\n      \"minLength\": 1,\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"normalized_target\": {\n      \"description\": \"Optional normalized path, URI, or identifier used by the verifier.\",\n      \"minLength\": 1,\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"notes\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"raw\": {\n      \"description\": \"Raw inline evidence token as written in a text-like artifact.\",\n      \"pattern\": \"^@evidence\\\\[\\\\[[^\\\\]]+\\\\]\\\\]$\",\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"schema_name\": {\n      \"const\": \"scientific-evidence.evidence-reference\"\n    },\n    \"target\": {\n      \"description\": \"Target path, URI, or stable identifier before the optional #locator.\",\n      \"minLength\": 1,\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"target_kind\": {\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"path\",\n        \"absolute-path\",\n        \"uri\",\n        \"id\",\n        \"unknown\"\n      ],\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"schema_name\",\n    \"raw\",\n    \"target\",\n    \"target_kind\"\n  ],\n  \"title\": \"Scientific Evidence Reference\",\n  \"type\": \"object\"\n}\n52:- `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\nRichFigureManifestFigure required ['figure_id', 'figure_type', 'files', 'panel_count', 'panels', 'uses_progression']\nfields ['attempted_generator', 'caption_short', 'estimated_min_rendered_font_pt', 'fallback_reason', 'figure_id', 'figure_type', 'files', 'font_sizes', 'generator', 'intended_latex_width_fraction', 'label', 'max_label_chars', 'min_font_size_pt', 'note', 'panel_count', 'panels', 'panels_per_row', 'placement', 'prompt_log_path', 'required', 'source_ids', 'uses_abbrev_labels', 'uses_progression']\nRichFigurePanel required None\nfields ['categories', 'category_count', 'content', 'label', 'metric', 'panel_id', 'series_count', 'task', 'threshold', 'y_range']\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Score-Targeted Item Selection for LLM Benchmark Prediction: Accuracy and Evaluation Limits\",\n  \"central_question\": \"What do grouped model holdouts reveal about fixed-prefix, source-only LLM benchmark prediction from 8, 16, or 32 responses, once measured prediction errors are distinguished from incomplete evaluations and failed controls?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"The score-covariance design with a group-tuned residual-profile/ridge predictor supports a conditional, heterogeneous description of few-item prediction on fixed benchmarks, but missing primary and baseline predictions and failed structural controls prevent a qualified overall accuracy or selection-advantage claim.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the final evaluation and its per-case predictions, not development scores. Its recorded aggregate MAE is 0.08801465854359276, whereas the uniform-selection fitted-predictor control records 0.044910877034389785; however, missing primary and baseline records receive worst-case error defaults. Therefore neither an improvement narrative nor a simple claim that learned selection worsens prediction is justified by that aggregate ordering. A standalone study can explain the delivered estimator, compare available matched predictions descriptively, and expose the evidence boundary. The evaluation completed but did not qualify, grounded independently in final.qualification.status, final.qualification.outcome, and final.qualification.correct. Incomplete records and failed structural checks are observed evaluator evidence; their underlying exception or defect is not identified. This is a bounded method evaluation and negative qualification result, not a demonstrated universal performance loss or a validated generalization result.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Fresh framing, not edit mode: no edit-context files, previous_paper manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory are present. The enum 'rejected' means that an inherited improvement/search-success storyline is rejected; it does not imply that an unseen prior manuscript was reviewed. Preservation and broadening of an actual prior paper are not applicable. Recenter the candidate story on final measured evidence and its limitations rather than importing the private memory's simple selection-reversal interpretation.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"paper_data.json.final_evaluation is independently usable although downstream_study is absent; it supplies the final qualification outcome and three final seeds with ten fixed benchmark universes and nested 8/16/32-item budgets.\",\n    \"The delivered selected-method source and METHOD.md directly document score-covariance acquisition, opaque-source-group tuning, Rasch-residual particles, a ridge fallback/blend, uniform-selection refitting, and a raw observed-mean control. They support mechanism description, not a claim that every fitted instance activated the particle component.\",\n    \"final.evidence.episodes changes the framing materially: commonsense primary predictions and the associated reference-baseline predictions are absent for seeds 5003 and 9001; several matched structural checks also fail there. These records must remain visible, not be silently dropped or called measured losses.\",\n    \"Inspection of task/evaluate_final.py establishes worst-case error defaults for missing records and a fallback paired interval. It does not expose the underlying exception responsible for the missing predictions. The final qualification facts themselves remain grounded in final.qualification.*.\",\n    \"No newly staged implementation family or prior paper requires cross-setting broadening. Open LLM Leaderboard-derived and HELM-derived cases are condition-specific evidence blocks under one shared binary-score prediction question, not distinct architectures or a transfer intervention.\",\n    \"No study.* grounding sources are present. Omit search score differences, selection-performance claims, and scored/unscored attempt counts rather than substituting private memory or result.* statistics. The delivered frozen method is the object of study, not the development trajectory.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Score-covariance selection with group-tuned residual-profile/ridge prediction\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The delivered method: a source-only nested item order, group-balanced design and particle priors, source-group-disjoint tuning, and a convex predictor with a linear fallback. Explain this family without attributing observed errors specifically to residual particles or asserting that the ridge fit itself is group-weighted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Uniform selection with refitted prediction\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The recorded no_diversity mode replaces the item order by a seeded uniform prefix and reruns the source-only fitting/tuning procedure. It tests a design-and-refitting intervention, not selection alone with a numerically identical predictor. Essential for interpreting the aggregate ordering and available-pair heterogeneity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Observed-mean controls under selected and uniform items\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The selected-item no_calibration mode is the raw queried-answer mean, not a clean removal of one affine calibration parameter. The evaluator's uniform baseline is a uniform-item sample mean, distinct from uniform selection with fitted prediction. These separate item choice from score reconstruction at matched query budgets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator anchor and two-parameter IRT references\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Retain the original anchor comparator wherever its predictions and the primary predictions are both available; include the recorded IRT reference as another meaningful comparator. The evaluator uses response-vector clustering/weighted representatives and a regularized two-parameter IRT implementation, respectively. Neither is established as an exact reproduction of a published package. Missing baselines are missing evidence, not observed MAE of one.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Source-mean prediction and observation-integrity controls\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Use source mean as a compact no-target-observation reference. Constant, copied, permuted, and replayed observations belong to the evidence-integrity analysis, not to a leaderboard of deployable methods. Keep their reported failures and the replay check distinct from estimation accuracy.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"First establish the evidence mask: for each benchmark, seed, condition, and reference method, distinguish available predictions, structural-check status, and missing outputs. The final evaluation completed but did not qualify. Failed primary records at commonsense seeds 5003 and 9001 and their missing baselines are essential main-text evidence.\",\n    \"Provide a main-text table of reported final aggregates with their scoring convention: primary MAE 0.08801465854359276, fitted uniform-selection control 0.044910877034389785, selected-item observed mean 0.10836174574059959, uniform sample mean 0.1335974367939037, IRT reference 0.1186668212839868, and anchor reference 0.22047382735923254. Clearly distinguish these evaluator-scored aggregates from measured-only accuracy; never turn the table into a superiority ranking.\",\n    \"Compare primary, fitted uniform selection, anchor, and IRT using available matched prediction records within each case and seed. Use the common availability intersection for the displayed methods and report support explicitly, including seeds omitted from a conditional calculation. Do not impute absent predictions, change the frozen comparator, or interpret conditioning as repairing qualification.\",\n    \"Separate the four Open LLM Leaderboard-derived cases (bbh, gpqa, musr, openllm_math) from the six HELM-derived cases (commonsense, gsm, legalbench, math, med_qa, mmlu), under one common question. Report descriptive heterogeneity rather than asserting a causal domain or architecture explanation.\",\n    \"Use stored per-target predictions and target_full_means to obtain budget-specific MAE and rank correlations for primary, fitted uniform selection, selected-item observed mean, and uniform sample mean on identical available support. Present budgets as experimental conditions, not development milestones. Do not assume error decreases monotonically.\",\n    \"Show all three final seed values where possible, with explicitly labeled descriptive ranges rather than invented confidence intervals. Target models and seeds overlap; benchmarks are fixed item universes, not independent draws from an item or benchmark population.\",\n    \"The stored candidate-minus-anchor contrast is -0.0769036132600842 with endpoints [-1,1]. The inspected evaluator initializes those endpoints when complete pairing is unavailable; this is not an informative empirical confidence interval or evidence of significance. Do not infer the contrast by subtracting the two separately reported aggregate MAEs, because missing-record defaults differ.\",\n    \"Explain what the controls can and cannot establish: recorded structural failures and incomplete predictions establish an evidence-integrity limitation, while final.metric.reset_max_abs_error=0.0 establishes only the checked replay identity. Neither identifies the underlying exception or validates every other gate. Do not use the unverified candidate diagnostics as independent evidence.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: source-only grouped fitting, fixed-prefix querying, residual-profile/ridge score reconstruction, and the distinction between observed prediction error and missing/failed evidence.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main comparison: two stacked panels of available matched MAE by benchmark, partitioned by data source, with seed-level points and explicit support annotations for four methods.\",\n    \"Figure 3, budget comparison: paired panels for MAE and Spearman correlation at 8, 16, and 32 items, comparing fitted prediction and raw observed means under selected and uniform items on common available support.\",\n    \"Main tables, not extra decorative figures: reported aggregate scores with default-penalty disclosure; a compact missing-record/structural-check audit. Keep these adjacent to conditional plots so the supplement cannot hide qualification failure.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"method_and_evidence_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the method and its evidential boundary understandable before the results; show that selection and tuning use source records only and final missing outputs are not prediction errors.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_flow\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; conceptual mechanism and evidence-status separation\",\n      \"split_by\": \"source fitting versus target observation versus evaluation\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty specifies one 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height; full width and column width coincide. Place a regular figure at 1.0 linewidth on page 1 or 2, approximately 5.5 by 1.9 inches plus caption. Use at most six short blocks in two rows, no more than three blocks per row, and at least 9-point final-size text. Visually separate the ridge and optional particle branches without suggesting a newly evaluated architecture. Do not add quantitative arrows implying improvement.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"available_pair_method_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Compare measured prediction error among primary, fitted uniform selection, anchor, and IRT while keeping incomplete records visible as unavailable evidence.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_dot_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
