{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_96","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"head -65 .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.py; ls .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts; /tmp/paper-venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nr=json.load(open('scientific-evidence/verification-report.json'));print(r.get('summary'))\nPY\n/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-\\\\section{Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability}\n+\\\\subsection{What a Fixed Prefix Can Identify}\n+The reconstruction problem has an information constraint independent of the chosen statistical model. If two target models have identical queried responses, the interface supplies no information that could distinguish them, so the estimator returns the same value \"'$a$. For their full scores $s_1$ and $s_2$, the triangle inequality gives\n+'\"\\\\begin{equation}\n+ |a-s_1|+|a-s_2|\\\\ \\\\geq\\\\ |s_1-s_2|.\n+\\\\end{equation}\n+Thus a fixed prefix cannot remove ambiguity among targets that agree on observed items but differ elsewhere. We do not measure this bound in the final evidence: the available score records do not provide the full queried response patterns needed for that analysis. The relation explains why source-level covariance is a design rationale rather than a target-level guarantee. A selector can only reduce ambiguities reflected in its training data; undocumented target response patterns may remain indistinguishable.\n+% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_method.py#L336-L365]]\n+\n+The two reconstruction branches make different assumptions about this unresolved information. The linear branch extends source score relationships through a regularized affine map. The profile branch assigns probability to source-shaped patterns with ability offsets, then averages their unobserved totals. The convex fallback prevents source tuning from requiring the profile branch, but does not protect against shifts absent from every validation group. Neither branch acquires new target information beyond the prescribed prefix. Increasing the number of particles or retuning the blend therefore cannot by itself resolve identical-prefix ambiguity. Conversely, replacing the item order may change which targets become indistinguishable, but its effect cannot be assigned to acquisition alone when the reconstruction is also refitted. This motivates keeping the procedure-level and matched-order comparisons separate in the results.\n+\n+\\\\section{Evaluation Protocol and Evidence Availability}\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
