{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_126","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-The reconstruction problem has an information constraint independent of the chosen statistical model. If two target models have identical queried responses, the interface supplies no information that could distinguish them, so the estimator returns the same value \"'$a$. For their full scores $s_1$ and $s_2$, the triangle inequality gives\n-'\"\\\\begin{equation}\n- |a-s_1|+|a-s_2|\\\\ \\\\geq\\\\ |s_1-s_2|.\n-\\\\end{equation}\n-Thus a fixed prefix cannot remove ambiguity among targets that agree on observed items but differ elsewhere. We do not measure this bound in the final evidence: the available score records do not provide the full queried response patterns needed for that analysis. The relation explains why source-level covariance is a design rationale rather than a target-level guarantee. A selector can only reduce ambiguities reflected in its training data; undocumented target response patterns may remain indistinguishable.\n+The reconstruction problem has an information constraint independent of the chosen statistical model. If two target models have identical queried responses, the interface supplies no information that could distinguish them, so the estimator returns the same value. In particular, neither target identities nor target group memberships are additional inputs to prediction. The source-trained mappings and particle priors are also identical for these rows. A fixed prefix therefore cannot distinguish targets that agree on observed items but differ elsewhere. We do not measure the prevalence of such pairs in the final evidence: the available score records do not supply the queried response patterns needed for that analysis. This source-inspectable property explains why score covariance is a design rationale rather than a target-level guarantee. Undocumented target response patterns may remain indistinguishable even when source-held-out prediction looks accurate.\n*** Update File: claims_grounding.json\n@@\n-      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Identical queried response rows receive the same estimator output. For any two such targets the sum of absolute errors is at least the absolute difference of their full scores, by the triangle inequality. This analytic observation is not measured on the final data and has no numerical empirical bound reported.\\\",\n+      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Identical queried response rows receive the same estimator output without target identity/group inputs. The prevalence of indistinguishable target pairs is not measured; this is a source-inspectable interface property, not a new theorem or empirical finding.\\\",\n*** Update File: build_paper_artifacts.py\n@@\n import json\n+import csv\n@@\n-from scipy.stats import spearmanr\n+from scipy.stats import spearmanr, kendalltau\n@@\n     support = {}\n     audit = []\n+    complete_rows = []\n+    primary_rows = []\n@@\n             case = episode['solution']['cases'][name]\n             truth = np.asarray(case['target_full_means'])\n+            for method in list(case['conditions']) + ['anchor', 'irt', 'uniform', 'source_mean']:\n+                values = predictions(case, method)\n+                condition = case['conditions'].get(method)\n+                for budget_index, budget in enumerate(episode['solution']['budgets']):\n+                    row = dict(case=name, seed=episode['seed'], budget=budget, method=method,\n+                               target_count=len(truth), target_group_count=len(set(case['target_lineages'])),\n+                               available=values is not None,\n+                               structural_failure=condition['structural_failure'] if condition else None,\n+                               mae=None, rmse=None, spearman=None, kendall_tau_b=None)\n+                    if values is not None:\n+                        predicted = values[budget_index]\n+                        row['mae'] = float(np.abs(predicted - truth).mean())\n+                        row['rmse'] = float(np.sqrt(np.mean((predicted - truth) ** 2)))\n+                        row['spearman'] = float(spearmanr(truth, predicted).statistic) if np.std(predicted) > 0 and np.std(truth) > 0 else 0.0\n+                        row['kendall_tau_b'] = float(kendalltau(truth, predicted).statistic) if np.std(predicted) > 0 and np.std(truth) > 0 else 0.0\n+                    complete_rows.append(row)\n+            primary = case['conditions']['primary']\n+            metrics_text = ' & '.join(f'{primary[metric]:.4f}' for metric in ['mae', 'rmse', 'spearman', 'kendall_tau_b']) if primary['completed'] else r'\\\\multicolumn{4}{c}{Missing; not a measured loss}'\n+            primary_rows.append(f'{LABELS[case_index]} & {episode[\\\"seed\\\"]} & {len(truth)} & {len(set(case[\\\"target_lineages\\\"]))} & {metrics_text}' + r' \\\\\\\\')\n@@\n     provenance(summary_path)\n+    complete_path = ROOT / 'complete_metrics.csv'\n+    with complete_path.open('w', newline='') as stream:\n+        writer = csv.DictWriter(stream, fieldnames=list(complete_rows[0]))\n+        writer.writeheader()\n+        writer.writerows(complete_rows)\n+    provenance(complete_path)\n@@\n     (TABLES / 'budget_metrics.tex').write_text('% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\\\n' + '\\\\n'.join(budget_rows) + '\\\\n')\n+    (TABLES / 'primary_seed_metrics.tex').write_text('% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\\\\n' + '\\\\n'.join(primary_rows) + '\\\\n')\n@@\n-        for stem in ['case_mae', 'budget_metrics']:\n+        for stem in ['case_mae', 'budget_metrics', 'primary_seed_metrics']:\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
